1. ECHILD / registration retirement

src/process.rs:190 — OwnedChild exclusively contains the private Tokio Child
and its group registration. Construction from an arbitrary pid, raw group
construction, mutable child access and extraction of the Child are private.
The SDK and pipe readers cannot acquire a child or a wait future.
src/process.rs:238 — retire_for_reap sends group SIGKILL, then retires the
signal-handler slot, before the only owned Child::wait. OwnedChild::drop uses
the same fence before Rust drops the private Child. A signal already holding
the slot finishes before retirement can release it. Existing slot-reuse tests
remain in the gate.
src/process.rs:196 — spawn refuses SIGCHLD=SIG_IGN and SA_NOCLDWAIT, before a
child exists. Both would invalidate the waitable-leader ownership premise.

Why the reported external-reaper window cannot arise through the supported
API: WNOWAIT does not reap; only the private wait/Drop can release this Child.
Pinned Tokio 1.54.1 process/unix/reap.rs polls its owned child; orphan.rs polls
only children handed to its orphan queue. Our Child reaches that queue only
after OwnedChild's Drop fence. rmcp 3.5.1 no longer receives a Child at all.
An unrelated OS process cannot waitpid our direct child. Foreign embedding
code calling unsafe waitpid on somebody else's child, or changing SIGCHLD to
automatic reaping after spawn, violates this explicit precondition. Observing
ECHILD cannot retroactively make such an external reap safe. The defensive
ECHILD branch disowns immediately; this is not a claim to solve that arbitrary
foreign-code case. The same assumption is in verification/contracts.toml.

Unit witness: echild_after_owned_reap_never_leaves_a_signal_target performs a
real owned wait, obtains real ECHILD from waitid afterward, inspects the actual
registry and records any signal after release. Removing the pre-wait fence
fails at “ECHILD observed a PID released while its group was registered”.
The signal-disposition witness also rejects both autoreap configurations.

2. MCP missing-id and failed-handoff cleanup

src/mcp.rs:450 — OwnedTransport wraps AsyncRwTransport with byte pipes and a
oneshot close notification. The supervisor retains the sole OwnedChild across
handshake, service completion and cleanup; the SDK cannot reap it.
src/mcp.rs:485 — finish_child retains rx's success/error result but performs
the same TERM/grace/owned wait in either case. Owned wait kills and retires
the group before reaping. There is no id()==None early return or raw-Child
handoff. A missing id can only follow an already fenced owned wait.

tests/mcp_client.rs:861 and :886 force both actual conditions, rather than
pretending they occurred: child.id()==None after a real wait, and an actual
oneshot receiver error after its sender is dropped. Each uses the real
stubborn_group server/member and asserts all original members reach ESRCH,
the registration is absent at reap, no late signal is issued, and a disposable
foreign process group remains alive until explicit fixture cleanup.

Foreign reuse is a deterministic fault injection: the audited group signal
callback routes a stale signal after the real leader's release to the fixture's
own disposable foreign group. The negative control actually kills that group
and trips each named foreign-signal assertion. This does not claim to force
Darwin's PID allocator to wrap. Group death and missing-id/receiver-error
observations use real processes and the actual private wait boundary.

